WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:23.099
There is no practical
obstacle whatever now to the

00:00:23.123 --> 00:00:28.040
creation of an efficient
index to all human knowledge,

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:30.400
ideas and achievements.

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:38.920
To the creation that is of a complete
planetary memory for all mankind.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.560
He was one of the early
inventors of science fiction.

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:47.600
The idea of time travel,

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:50.200
the possibility
of invisibility...

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:51.720
..of inter-competence.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:56.040
galactic struggles.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:01.954
And then he came up with ideas
of how we might reorganise the

00:01:01.978 --> 00:01:05.800
knowledge apparatus of the world
which he called the world brain.

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:09.800
For Wells the world brain
had to contain all that was...

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:15.360
learned unknown and that
was being learned unknown.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:19.240
If you have access to
anything that's been written,

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:20.960
not just theoretical access,

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:23.920
but like instant access
next to your brain,

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:26.800
that changes your
idea of who you are.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:32.400
It can be reproduced
exactly and fully in Peru,

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:33.840
China,

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:35.280
Iceland,

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:36.960
Central Africa,

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:38.920
or wherever else.

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:46.560
They were frank in their ambition and
dazzling in their ability to execute it.

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:48.040
The

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:52.378
Google Book Scanning
Project is clearly the most

00:01:52.402 --> 00:01:56.760
ambitious brain scheme
that has ever been invented.

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:01.640
This is no remote dream,

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:02.320
no fantasy.

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:07.560
It is a plain statement of a
contemporary state of affairs.

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:13.520
There is a danger that Google's
aim is to achieve a monopoly

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:18.680
The nightmare
scenario in 20 years'

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:21.040
time would be Google
tracking everything we read.

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:25.040
Google could basically
hold the whole world hostage.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:27.040
since

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:27.560
Wells.

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:31.219
Science fiction is always
about the possibility that people

00:02:31.243 --> 00:02:33.895
don't really matter in the
future and the plot is always

00:02:33.919 --> 00:02:36.547
about some heroic person
that either succeeds or doesn't

00:02:36.571 --> 00:02:39.200
succeed in proving that
people really matter after all.

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:55.880
It's a library,

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:57.200
a public library,

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:01.160
where people go to look at books
and read them and take them away.

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:05.728
That girl works at the library
and she checks on books

00:03:05.752 --> 00:03:08.560
that are going out and
books that are coming back in.

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:10.120
I love libraries.

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:11.400
I like the smell,

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:15.280
the smell of paper
properly preserved.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:18.974
It's as if it's the smell of a
hay barn that's been cleared

00:03:18.998 --> 00:03:22.080
of all its animals and made
into human intelligence.

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:23.480
And in a library,

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:24.320
you really,

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:25.080
even if you're sitting...

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:25.960
Sitting in the tea room,

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:28.760
discussing your latest findings,

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:32.302
it's amazing how much
social interaction with other

00:03:32.326 --> 00:03:35.800
people will actually help you
to enrich what you're doing.

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:37.600
In this part of the library,

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:40.160
the grown-ups can read
the stories to the children.

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:41.680
People sometimes say to me,

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:43.680
aren't libraries obsolete?

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:46.320
It's absurd.

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:48.520
They are nerve centres,

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:50.240
centres of intellectual energy.

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:52.480
Libraries stand for an ideal,

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:54.720
which is an educated public.

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:56.960
And to the degree that
knowledge is power,

00:03:57.320 --> 00:04:00.138
they also stand
therefore for the idea that

00:04:00.162 --> 00:04:03.520
power should be disseminated
and not centralized.

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:29.400
The first appeal of

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:37.760
Google's enterprise when we saw it was just
digitizing millions and millions of books.

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:42.680
At Harvard we have by far
the greatest university library.

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:43.440
in the world.

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:44.520
It's enormous,

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:46.480
17 million volumes,

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:52.520
and every library wants its
holdings digitized for lots of reasons,

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:53.720
including preservation.

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:55.200
But beyond that,

00:04:56.120 --> 00:05:00.000
it raises the possibility of
sharing your intellectual wealth.

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:03.880
I think of the Harvard Library
as an international asset,

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:08.880
something that should be opened up
and shared with the general population.

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:11.440
So here comes Google,

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:12.640
they've got the energy,

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:14.040
they've got the technology,

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:16.600
they've got the money and
they said we'll do it for you,

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:17.520
free.

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:21.680
Google did such a fabulous
job in creating a vision,

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:26.160
not only that a universal
digital library could be created,

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:28.360
but that it could be done today,

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:29.680
the Google.

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:30.680
engineers,

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:33.880
are like good
engineers everywhere.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:37.440
They just like to think about how
do we surmount these challenges.

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:42.960
They sort of leave the lawsuit
to the lawyers to worry about.

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:52.864
Google's a company that believes
in its fundamental mission of

00:05:52.888 --> 00:05:57.600
empowering everyone in this world
with all the information they need.

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:00.320
Enriched with the
right information,

00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:01.720
people can make

00:06:01.880 --> 00:06:03.480
better decisions for themselves,

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:04.480
their families,

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:05.640
and their communities.

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:11.440
This world is full of wonderful
individuals which have varied needs,

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:13.320
from a farmer in Africa,

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:15.200
to a mother in India,

00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:17.440
to a business person in Japan.

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:21.200
Everyone needs information
in this modern day and age.

00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:24.254
And Google believes
in breaking all the barrier

00:06:24.278 --> 00:06:27.560
between every individual
and the information they seek.

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:31.640
When you actually
negotiate with Google,

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:35.320
and do so on their turf,

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:38.240
you enter a strange world.

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:43.440
A Google office doesn't
have chairs like this chair.

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:51.440
The furniture consists of large inflated
balls that are colored green or red or yellow,

00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:54.880
and the young Google
engineers are sitting on these.

00:06:54.920 --> 00:06:56.960
It's a kind of Never
Never Land feeling.

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:01.280
About ten years ago,

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:06.508
I got a visit from the vice
president of Google and she walked

00:07:06.532 --> 00:07:10.960
into my office and described a
project that Google had in mind,

00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:15.320
which was to digitize all the
books in the Harvard Library.

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:18.000
My first thought was,

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:19.400
To put it bluntly,

00:07:19.480 --> 00:07:20.920
that maybe they were
smoking something,

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:22.200
because I didn't
think it was possible.

00:07:23.720 --> 00:07:26.720
Harvard had been digitizing
books from time to time,

00:07:27.160 --> 00:07:28.800
but there were very
limited numbers,

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:30.560
and we didn't do many.

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:34.480
It was a very expensive
and complicated project.

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:36.000
I don't remember exactly,

00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:38.800
but it was several hundred
dollars just for a single book.

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:48.560
But they had invented a copying station
that was a lot cheaper and easier to use.

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:51.280
that didn't damage the books,

00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:53.640
or at least went out of its
way not to damage the books.

00:07:54.200 --> 00:07:56.720
And it seemed to me that
it had a lot of plausibility.

00:07:57.480 --> 00:08:00.120
And so we decided
to give it a try.

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:03.080
Every great
library did digitizing,

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:04.600
sometimes on a large scale.

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:09.480
Our Open Collections program
digitized 2.3 million pages.

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:09.720
I mean,

00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:10.520
that's big,

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:14.000
but nothing like as big as what

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:15.680
Google attempted to do.

00:08:16.280 --> 00:08:18.240
the sheer ambition of

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:19.800
digitizing everything.

00:08:30.920 --> 00:08:32.000
In the ancient world,

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:36.249
At the Library of Alexandria
they copied rolls and

00:08:36.273 --> 00:08:40.120
tablets and in an attempt
to copy all that was known.

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:44.720
And eventually the library
was destroyed by Julius Caesar.

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:51.640
And the loss of the Library of Alexandria
was an international catastrophe.

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:56.160
The Universal Library has
been talked about for millennia.

00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:59.677
There's a kind of a continuity
of development and we mustn't

00:08:59.701 --> 00:09:02.480
forget the important role
that libraries and scholars

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:06.280
have always made
for millennia of copying.

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:09.680
And then you see with
the development of printing,

00:09:10.040 --> 00:09:11.720
the multiplicity of texts,

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:13.800
the copying of original texts.

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:18.348
It was possible to think in the
Renaissance that you might be able to amass

00:09:18.372 --> 00:09:22.560
the whole of published knowledge
in a single room or a single institution.

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:32.000
I have here a monument to French
thought - the Great Encyclopedia

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:34.948
Among the various famous
philosophers involved in this

00:09:34.972 --> 00:09:37.520
project, the two leaders
were Diderot and D'Alembert

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:42.480
They wanted to collect all
the knowledge of the day

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:48.000
and put it at the service of freedom
of thought and liberty of conscience

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:52.480
The encyclopedia embodies a
moment of French consciousness

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:56.800
in the middle of the eighteenth
century, at the end of Absolute Monarchy

00:09:57.200 --> 00:10:02.160
It paved the way for the intellectual
movement which led to the French Revolution

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:05.360
Then in the

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:06.560
19th century,

00:10:06.680 --> 00:10:09.650
you have various suggestions
in France and Belgium

00:10:09.674 --> 00:10:12.400
that you can create a
catalog of everything.

00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:14.360
What will come
next is microfilm.

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:18.240
And so you start finding
huge microfilming projects.

00:10:18.360 --> 00:10:19.200
And so for us,

00:10:19.240 --> 00:10:23.840
the Google project was a sort of a natural
extension of that process of development.

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:32.840
Project Gutenberg of Michael
Hart was the first digital library.

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:34.840
He started on the
4th of July in early

00:10:35.520 --> 00:10:38.003
1970s going and typing the
Declaration of Independence

00:10:38.027 --> 00:10:40.040
so that everybody
could have access to it.

00:10:40.360 --> 00:10:44.320
Thousands of volunteers worked from
all over the world to go and build this.

00:10:44.480 --> 00:10:47.935
He even had the idea that it
ought to be possible to download

00:10:47.959 --> 00:10:51.240
the entire library that he
had created if you wanted it.

00:10:51.320 --> 00:10:55.280
And I think it did act as a kind
of example of something that

00:10:55.640 --> 00:10:56.200
Later on,

00:10:56.440 --> 00:10:58.600
Google and others
took up in a much bigger,

00:10:59.080 --> 00:11:01.880
more extensive way.

00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:09.120
My name is Raymond
Kurzweil and I'm from Queens,

00:11:09.160 --> 00:11:09.480
New York.

00:11:09.480 --> 00:11:10.480
When I was 12,

00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:13.240
I became fascinated
with pattern recognition.

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:14.760
And as a young teenager,

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:19.760
I did a project to teach computers
how to recognize patterns in music.

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:24.000
I built a computer and by feeding
in certain relationships in music,

00:11:24.120 --> 00:11:24.360
I was...

00:11:24.560 --> 00:11:26.080
Able to write music with him.

00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:26.240
Raymond,

00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:26.680
how old are you?

00:11:26.720 --> 00:11:28.280
I'm 17.

00:11:28.280 --> 00:11:29.440
Do your parents know
what you've been up to?

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:33.577
Recognizing printed letters
was a classical unsolved

00:11:33.601 --> 00:11:36.120
problem in the field
of pattern recognition.

00:11:36.680 --> 00:11:41.080
And so I created the first OmniFont
optical character recognition.

00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:43.120
This was around 1975.

00:11:43.160 --> 00:11:46.280
In 1978 we developed
a commercial version.

00:11:46.520 --> 00:11:52.120
And we talked about how you could ultimately
scan all books and all printed material.

00:11:53.360 --> 00:11:55.480
When automobiles
came along first,

00:11:55.760 --> 00:11:58.480
they seemed likely to
become a rich man's monopoly.

00:11:59.120 --> 00:12:01.440
They cost upwards
of a thousand pounds.

00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:04.280
Henry Ford ought to be all that.

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:06.880
He put the poor man on the road.

00:12:07.840 --> 00:12:11.920
We want a Henry Ford today to
modernize the distribution of knowledge,

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:14.760
make good knowledge
cheap and easy,

00:12:15.280 --> 00:12:17.360
in this still very ignorant,

00:12:17.560 --> 00:12:18.480
ill-educated,

00:12:18.600 --> 00:12:19.400
ill-served,

00:12:19.800 --> 00:12:21.640
English-speaking world of ours,

00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:26.960
which might be the greatest power
on earth for the good of mankind.

00:12:37.240 --> 00:12:40.200
We started the
Internet Archive in 1996.

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:46.960
The idea was to have all the published
works of humankind available to everybody.

00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:49.760
That this was the
opportunity of our generation.

00:12:50.280 --> 00:12:50.480
That,

00:12:50.960 --> 00:12:52.440
like the previous generation,

00:12:52.480 --> 00:12:53.640
it put a man on the moon.

00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:57.240
The Internet Archive had been
completely open with Google.

00:12:57.240 --> 00:12:57.600
In fact,

00:12:57.640 --> 00:13:00.360
I'd gone and given a
speech that was attended by...

00:13:00.880 --> 00:13:03.580
I think all of the senior
executives on how one could

00:13:03.604 --> 00:13:06.200
go about building a
digital library of all books,

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:06.880
video.

00:13:07.160 --> 00:13:09.480
And I had hoped that there was
going to be a way to work with them,

00:13:10.440 --> 00:13:11.360
but that was not to be.

00:13:12.360 --> 00:13:14.360
Libraries had signed secret
agreements with Google.

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:16.560
We didn't know what
was really going on.

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:18.908
When they started
coming out as a completely

00:13:18.932 --> 00:13:20.840
separate project and
not working with others,

00:13:21.280 --> 00:13:23.800
then I started to
become suspicious.

00:13:25.640 --> 00:13:26.440
Larry Page,

00:13:26.720 --> 00:13:28.040
who founded Google with me,

00:13:28.560 --> 00:13:31.368
first proposed that
we digitize all books a

00:13:31.392 --> 00:13:34.200
decade ago when we
were a fledgling startup.

00:13:35.080 --> 00:13:36.080
Five years later,

00:13:36.320 --> 00:13:37.360
in 2004,

00:13:37.840 --> 00:13:39.160
Google Books was born.

00:13:40.080 --> 00:13:43.240
Despite a number of important
digitization efforts to date,

00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:46.808
none have been at a comparable
scale simply because no

00:13:46.832 --> 00:13:50.040
one else has chosen to
invest the requisite resources.

00:13:50.840 --> 00:13:52.600
If Google Books is successful,

00:13:53.120 --> 00:13:54.080
others will follow.

00:13:54.720 --> 00:13:57.760
I don't think that Google is aware
of the fact that it's a corporation.

00:13:58.560 --> 00:14:03.960
I think Google does think of itself as an
NGO that just happens to make a lot of money.

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:17.160
And they think of themselves as social reformers who just happen to have their stock
traded on stock exchanges and who just happen to have investors and shareholders,

00:14:17.240 --> 00:14:21.800
but they do think of themselves as ultimately
being in the business of making the world better.

00:14:27.760 --> 00:14:32.120
There are few more irreparable
property losses than vanished books.

00:14:33.080 --> 00:14:33.480
Nature,

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:34.960
politics,

00:14:35.480 --> 00:14:38.880
and war have always been the
mortal enemies of written works.

00:14:39.880 --> 00:14:40.520
Most recently,

00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:44.680
Hurricane Katrina dealt a blow
to the libraries of the Gulf Coast.

00:14:45.720 --> 00:14:46.840
At Tulane University,

00:14:46.880 --> 00:14:50.440
the main library sat
in nine feet of water.

00:14:51.680 --> 00:14:51.960
In the

00:14:52.800 --> 00:14:52.920
1970s,

00:14:53.200 --> 00:14:55.688
The Khmer Rouge regime
in Cambodia decimated

00:14:55.712 --> 00:14:58.200
cultural institutions
throughout the country.

00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:00.000
Khmer Rouge fighters...

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:01.800
...took over the
National Library,

00:15:02.280 --> 00:15:03.960
throwing the books
into the street,

00:15:04.640 --> 00:15:05.160
burning them,

00:15:05.720 --> 00:15:08.040
while using the
stacks as a pigsty.

00:15:09.200 --> 00:15:09.920
Now with Google,

00:15:10.560 --> 00:15:13.888
the University of Michigan
is involved in one of the

00:15:13.912 --> 00:15:17.240
most extensive preservation
projects in world history.

00:15:17.880 --> 00:15:22.280
Google Books is a potent
idea on a number of dimensions.

00:15:22.680 --> 00:15:27.880
What I like about Google
Books is the idea of...

00:15:28.320 --> 00:15:29.320
Not losing books,

00:15:29.400 --> 00:15:33.120
especially books that might
be genuinely abandoned.

00:15:33.440 --> 00:15:35.920
The idea of getting
all that stuff online is,

00:15:36.280 --> 00:15:36.760
of course,

00:15:36.800 --> 00:15:37.640
going to be a benefit.

00:15:37.880 --> 00:15:39.920
So that we have to love.

00:16:02.320 --> 00:16:05.960
I went to Google
in January 2003.

00:16:05.960 --> 00:16:07.320
I actually made what now

00:16:07.680 --> 00:16:09.040
I feel quite embarrassed about.

00:16:09.880 --> 00:16:11.440
I made a presentation to them,

00:16:12.200 --> 00:16:13.760
telling them what
they ought to be doing,

00:16:14.400 --> 00:16:16.576
only to find out a few
months later that they'd

00:16:16.600 --> 00:16:18.680
actually been doing
it for a while already.

00:16:20.600 --> 00:16:23.280
Project Ocean was
the kind of code name,

00:16:23.800 --> 00:16:24.920
development code name,

00:16:24.960 --> 00:16:27.280
that Google were giving
to what eventually became

00:16:27.640 --> 00:16:28.280
Google Books.

00:16:29.160 --> 00:16:31.240
So it was called Project
Ocean because it was big,

00:16:31.600 --> 00:16:32.080
I imagine.

00:16:35.440 --> 00:16:39.560
Google seemed to think that they
could do almost a million in three years.

00:17:05.040 --> 00:17:09.819
You could say that this
mass digitization is something

00:17:09.843 --> 00:17:14.080
like running a huge
machine through a library.

00:17:14.920 --> 00:17:16.800
You take books by the shelf,

00:17:18.040 --> 00:17:19.640
they're put in cartons.

00:17:19.960 --> 00:17:20.680
on carts,

00:17:21.280 --> 00:17:22.560
they're loaded onto trucks,

00:17:23.320 --> 00:17:23.640
and then

00:17:24.280 --> 00:17:31.000
Google at this time had three places in the
country where it was doing digitization.

00:17:32.160 --> 00:17:34.800
Supposedly it didn't
give the address.

00:17:34.880 --> 00:17:35.600
of where they were.

00:17:35.720 --> 00:17:39.280
Google won't say how much
scanning all the books cost,

00:17:39.400 --> 00:17:44.240
but there are estimates that it's
somewhere between $30 and $100 per book.

00:17:44.560 --> 00:17:47.440
So if you multiply that times,

00:17:47.640 --> 00:17:48.760
$20 million.

00:17:48.760 --> 00:17:52.348
Google early on bent
over backwards to keep us

00:17:52.372 --> 00:17:55.960
from communicating
with the other libraries.

00:17:55.960 --> 00:17:57.720
There were three
or four large ones.

00:17:58.080 --> 00:18:04.600
and each of us was told we should not tell
the others what kind of a contract we had.

00:18:04.720 --> 00:18:06.160
and how we were
working with Google.

00:18:06.360 --> 00:18:06.920
To begin with,

00:18:06.920 --> 00:18:09.040
it had to be kept fairly quiet.

00:18:10.000 --> 00:18:16.320
It was probably mid-2003 when I
started to take the wraps off in terms of,

00:18:16.920 --> 00:18:20.840
this is going to be a possibility that
we might be working with Google.

00:18:23.280 --> 00:18:26.000
I witnessed the scale of the
operation and it was very impressive.

00:18:27.120 --> 00:18:32.245
20 very large workstations
with very high resolution

00:18:32.269 --> 00:18:38.200
cameras sitting on top of
cradle with very intense lights.

00:18:39.480 --> 00:18:42.889
and underneath a lot of
black boxes which presumably

00:18:42.913 --> 00:18:46.120
contained all of Google's
algorithms that makes

00:18:46.560 --> 00:18:50.400
Google search what it is and
they uploaded that stuff straight to

00:18:51.240 --> 00:18:53.080
Mountain View straight from

00:18:53.280 --> 00:18:54.720
Mozart.

00:18:54.800 --> 00:18:57.310
Google certainly depends on
knowing more and more and more

00:18:57.334 --> 00:18:59.800
for their algorithm to be
better and better and better.

00:19:00.960 --> 00:19:05.200
And this is the core of the way
economics in this space now works.

00:19:06.960 --> 00:19:12.508
They had a specific interest in having
lots of things in Google that would lead

00:19:12.532 --> 00:19:18.080
people to use Google so they could
make money by having advertisements there.

00:19:20.480 --> 00:19:21.120
What are books?

00:19:21.200 --> 00:19:22.240
They're full of data.

00:19:22.520 --> 00:19:22.920
And so.

00:19:23.000 --> 00:19:25.360
So the more data you have,

00:19:25.440 --> 00:19:28.880
the more you can fine tune
your search technologies.

00:19:31.920 --> 00:19:34.760
Some of the enthusiasts for

00:19:35.560 --> 00:19:37.360
Google's way of gathering data,

00:19:37.560 --> 00:19:38.800
and it's not just Google at all,

00:19:38.840 --> 00:19:39.120
I mean,

00:19:39.520 --> 00:19:41.080
it's Silicon Valley in general.

00:19:41.160 --> 00:19:43.520
It's the current
cultural moment.

00:19:43.560 --> 00:19:45.440
It includes the other
Silicon Valley companies,

00:19:45.480 --> 00:19:47.320
but also the modern
world of finance,

00:19:47.320 --> 00:19:50.600
and and also the modern
world of spycraft for states,

00:19:50.640 --> 00:19:52.440
and also the modern
world of criminality.

00:19:52.920 --> 00:19:55.840
in the modern world of
insurance and healthcare.

00:19:56.480 --> 00:20:00.000
All these things have this idea
that you grab all this data in order to

00:20:00.040 --> 00:20:01.240
You become very powerful.

00:20:01.280 --> 00:20:04.748
You create a differential in your
ability to see information versus the

00:20:04.772 --> 00:20:08.240
ordinary person and you create
these new incredible castles of power.

00:20:08.840 --> 00:20:09.960
But it's okay.

00:20:10.160 --> 00:20:11.857
It's not just traditional
power mongering

00:20:11.881 --> 00:20:13.920
because you're making
the world more efficient.

00:20:24.920 --> 00:20:28.742
I was a little boy in the
70s growing up in India

00:20:28.766 --> 00:20:32.348
