00:39:56.920 --> 00:39:57.320
First,

00:39:58.040 --> 00:40:00.040
we learned that Google
was scanning books.

00:40:00.440 --> 00:40:01.920
And I remember loving that idea,

00:40:02.280 --> 00:40:03.280
because I'm a reader,

00:40:03.840 --> 00:40:05.480
and I write nonfiction books,

00:40:05.480 --> 00:40:06.400
and I do research,

00:40:06.640 --> 00:40:06.920
and

00:40:07.400 --> 00:40:09.080
I wanted access to those books.

00:40:09.920 --> 00:40:13.080
Then we heard that they
were scanning our books.

00:40:13.320 --> 00:40:14.720
They were scanning
copyrighted books,

00:40:14.880 --> 00:40:16.560
and they hadn't asked
anyone's permission.

00:40:16.920 --> 00:40:18.560
The libraries had
just handed them over.

00:40:18.640 --> 00:40:18.800
Well,

00:40:20.040 --> 00:40:22.680
that was obviously a
violation of our copyrights,

00:40:23.040 --> 00:40:25.480
and a little bit of a surprise.

00:40:26.280 --> 00:40:26.960
Put it mildly.

00:40:27.360 --> 00:40:29.640
I remember being very curious
about what they were doing,

00:40:30.200 --> 00:40:37.200
and I popped my name into Google and saw
that it came up with snippets of my book.

00:40:37.360 --> 00:40:41.400
So what I did was I searched for terms
that I knew were common in the book,

00:40:42.120 --> 00:40:43.800
like star or galaxy.

00:40:44.640 --> 00:40:46.480
And there were
lots and lots of hits,

00:40:46.800 --> 00:40:48.880
and it would display
several snippets.

00:40:48.920 --> 00:40:49.160
And then

00:40:50.000 --> 00:40:51.960
I would search for
other common words.

00:40:52.040 --> 00:40:54.800
And it was clear that if you
were clever about your searches,

00:40:54.800 --> 00:40:55.280
you could see

00:40:55.520 --> 00:40:56.280
quite a bit of the text,

00:40:56.280 --> 00:40:56.960
if not all of it.

00:40:57.480 --> 00:41:02.240
The problem that most
authors have is obscurity.

00:41:03.000 --> 00:41:04.200
That's the issue.

00:41:04.200 --> 00:41:05.960
There are a gazillion books.

00:41:06.720 --> 00:41:08.960
How do you get people
to pay attention to yours?

00:41:09.320 --> 00:41:13.782
Google claimed that its use
of these millions of copyrighted

00:41:13.806 --> 00:41:18.040
books that it had digitized
was an example of fair use.

00:41:18.840 --> 00:41:19.200
Why?

00:41:19.400 --> 00:41:20.000
I'm not sure.

00:41:20.240 --> 00:41:23.280
I still don't understand
how that can be justified.

00:41:23.760 --> 00:41:24.520
The point is that.

00:41:24.760 --> 00:41:26.280
The entire book has been copied,

00:41:26.640 --> 00:41:29.568
and it's been copied by
a single company that's

00:41:29.592 --> 00:41:32.520
doing it for purposes
of profiting off the work.

00:41:33.160 --> 00:41:37.120
If you allow a profit-making
company to copy a million books,

00:41:37.640 --> 00:41:40.164
then how can you say
no to the next enterprise

00:41:40.188 --> 00:41:42.600
that also wants to
copy the million books?

00:41:43.200 --> 00:41:48.640
So the Authors Guild organized a class
action suit asking them to stop doing that.

00:41:49.640 --> 00:41:53.800
The Authors Guild on Tuesday filed a
lawsuit against search engine Google.

00:41:54.200 --> 00:41:56.948
