01:13:34.120 --> 01:13:36.624
The court's decision
was to a considerable

01:13:36.648 --> 01:13:39.520
extent going to determine
the future of books,

01:13:39.720 --> 01:13:41.040
of digital books.

01:13:42.160 --> 01:13:45.965
The proposed settlement
results in a de facto monopoly on

01:13:45.989 --> 01:13:50.680
information and an intensification
of media concentration on Google.

01:13:51.520 --> 01:13:52.200
As a result,

01:13:52.480 --> 01:13:54.840
the right of free
access to information,

01:13:55.280 --> 01:13:57.680
as well as the existing
cultural diversity,

01:13:58.240 --> 01:13:59.840
in both Germany and Europe,

01:14:00.240 --> 01:14:01.160
will be usurped.

01:14:01.560 --> 01:14:05.238
Would it be basically in
the hands of commercial

01:14:05.262 --> 01:14:09.960
speculators whose responsibility
was to their shareholders?

01:14:10.640 --> 01:14:12.600
Or would it be organized
for the public good?

01:14:13.200 --> 01:14:18.680
There was a risk of monopolization
there that the Department of Justice saw.

01:14:20.240 --> 01:14:24.928
The proposed settlement would
establish a marketplace in which only

01:14:24.952 --> 01:14:29.640
one competitor would have
authority to use a vast array of works.

01:14:29.800 --> 01:14:31.480
The risk was that

01:14:32.520 --> 01:14:36.880
Google could basically hold
the whole world hostage to...

01:14:37.640 --> 01:14:43.880
The price of access to these books and
because no one else would have a license,

01:14:43.920 --> 01:14:47.240
no one else would have a
corpus like the corpus they had,

01:14:47.840 --> 01:14:51.200
we'd have to pay whatever
they wanted to charge.

01:14:52.640 --> 01:14:55.054
The core concerns
seem to be that this would

01:14:55.078 --> 01:14:58.000
diminish the availability
to read books in private.

01:14:59.000 --> 01:15:00.160
That is not true.

01:15:00.960 --> 01:15:03.880
This service would be
available at public libraries.

01:15:04.760 --> 01:15:06.600
You can walk into your
neighborhood library.

01:15:07.080 --> 01:15:09.240
You can sit down at
a free access terminal,

01:15:09.800 --> 01:15:10.480
anonymously.

01:15:11.080 --> 01:15:13.080
You can search
for and read a book.

01:15:14.200 --> 01:15:15.800
And if you want to
look at it at home,

01:15:16.160 --> 01:15:16.640
then what?

01:15:17.320 --> 01:15:17.560
Well,

01:15:17.800 --> 01:15:19.280
if you want to
look at it at home,

01:15:19.400 --> 01:15:20.960
that may present an issue.

01:15:22.560 --> 01:15:23.120
Here's the rub.

01:15:24.000 --> 01:15:27.968
This is a tension between
requirements for security that are insisted

01:15:27.992 --> 01:15:31.960
on in order not to have these
works be sort of freely disseminated.

01:15:34.440 --> 01:15:35.040
In my view...

01:15:35.560 --> 01:15:40.078
The Google Books search
settlement is no different from the piracy

01:15:40.102 --> 01:15:44.480
cases in which the internet and
digital technology are abused.

01:15:45.000 --> 01:15:48.320
I strongly urge the court to
reject the proposed settlement.

01:15:49.320 --> 01:15:52.080
I remember there being
a Japanese writer there,

01:15:52.480 --> 01:15:55.520
and the language was very vivid.

01:15:55.560 --> 01:15:56.600
It was as though,

01:15:57.120 --> 01:15:57.400
you know,

01:15:57.520 --> 01:16:01.160
as copyright was
going to be swept away,

01:16:01.320 --> 01:16:03.760
and that copyright was
going to be destroyed.

01:16:04.960 --> 01:16:06.880
approval of the
settlement was going to,

01:16:07.560 --> 01:16:07.880
you know,

01:16:09.560 --> 01:16:12.680
make the United States out of
compliance with treaty obligations.

01:16:14.120 --> 01:16:17.640
There's a real risk that should
the court approve the settlement,

01:16:18.160 --> 01:16:21.008
members of the World
Trade Organization will

01:16:21.032 --> 01:16:24.280
initiate settlement
proceedings against the U.S.

01:16:24.360 --> 01:16:24.920
government.

01:16:25.880 --> 01:16:26.840
And if the U.S.

01:16:26.920 --> 01:16:29.000
government were to
lose such proceedings,

01:16:29.680 --> 01:16:31.640
which is a very
real possibility,

01:16:32.280 --> 01:16:37.560
Our partners would be entitled to impose
trade sanctions against the United States.

01:16:38.040 --> 01:16:40.200
You don't use words
like that very often.

01:16:40.240 --> 01:16:41.200
It wasn't kind of like,

01:16:41.560 --> 01:16:42.040
oh gee,

01:16:42.040 --> 01:16:44.360
there are these issues and
we're concerned about something.

01:16:44.400 --> 01:16:45.000
It was like,

01:16:45.600 --> 01:16:46.760
this violates a treaty

01:16:47.400 --> 01:16:50.280
How can the judge do something
that's going to violate a treaty?

01:16:50.360 --> 01:16:51.200
This is crazy.

01:16:51.960 --> 01:16:53.480
I am not going to rule today.

01:16:54.000 --> 01:16:55.720
There's just too much to digest.

01:16:56.360 --> 01:16:57.720
I will reserve decision.

01:16:58.640 --> 01:16:59.880
There's much to think about.

01:17:00.640 --> 01:17:01.240
Arise.

01:17:02.320 --> 01:17:04.640
And then Judge
Chin thought about it.

01:17:05.120 --> 01:17:06.040
He thought about it,

01:17:06.400 --> 01:17:07.440
and he thought about it.

01:17:07.960 --> 01:17:11.992
Google is a flagship American
company which makes

01:17:12.016 --> 01:17:15.880
a lot of money for
America all over the world

01:17:16.600 --> 01:17:24.600
and so we were worried that the court might
deliver a politically-motivated judgement

01:17:26.320 --> 01:17:28.440
He took a very long time.

01:17:28.640 --> 01:17:30.320
Every morning I
got up and I thought,

01:17:30.840 --> 01:17:31.200
what is...

01:17:31.440 --> 01:17:33.960
Is Judge Chin going to
announce his decision today?

01:17:34.520 --> 01:17:35.640
And when he finally did,

01:17:36.000 --> 01:17:42.440
I myself felt thrilled because the court
actually refused to sanction the settlement.

01:17:43.240 --> 01:17:46.360
Then Google Book
Search could not take place,

01:17:46.480 --> 01:17:49.160
at least according to
Google's original business plan.

01:17:49.960 --> 01:17:50.160
U.S.

01:17:50.240 --> 01:17:53.627
Circuit Judge Denny Chin
said the creation of the Universal

01:17:53.651 --> 01:17:56.920
Library would benefit many
but would simply go too far.

01:17:57.360 --> 01:18:01.200
Chin said the settlement of a class action
lawsuit that the company reached with U.S.

01:18:01.280 --> 01:18:04.511
authors and publishers would
grant Google significant rights

01:18:04.535 --> 01:18:08.040
to exploit entire books without
permission of copyright owners.

01:18:08.480 --> 01:18:11.038
Chin also said the deal
gives Google a significant

01:18:11.062 --> 01:18:14.040
advantage over competitors
and it would be rewarding it.

01:18:14.240 --> 01:18:17.720
for engaging in wholesale copying of
copyrighted works without permission.

01:18:17.920 --> 01:18:22.320
expected that this would be
the outcome. I wasn't surpris

01:18:22.640 --> 01:18:27.360
Of course I was nappy
that it turned out this way but

01:18:27.760 --> 01:18:31.360
I didn't crack open the
champagne or anything like that

01:18:32.240 --> 01:18:37.200
I don't know Judge
Chin, but I salute him

01:18:37.760 --> 01:18:42.560
as an example of the remarkable
independence of the American judicial system

01:18:42.960 --> 01:18:47.120
Despite all the formidable
financial power of Google

01:18:47.520 --> 01:18:51.920
he quietly ruled that the agreements
drawn up by this Mountain View firm

01:18:52.320 --> 01:18:57.280
were invalid, baseless
and inappropriate

01:18:58.320 --> 01:19:01.888
I think you could read
the decision by Judge

01:19:01.912 --> 01:19:05.480
Chen as a defeat of
the screen by the book.

01:19:05.840 --> 01:19:08.680
But this is a long war.

01:19:09.640 --> 01:19:10.760
This is one battle.

01:19:10.920 --> 01:19:12.320
and um

01:19:13.280 --> 01:19:16.888
Whatever triumph there might
have been for books is going to

01:19:16.912 --> 01:19:20.520
be short-lived because the
screen will ultimately triumph.

01:19:27.800 --> 01:19:31.360
They spent several months
trying to negotiate a new settlement,

01:19:32.080 --> 01:19:35.320
couldn't reach a new settlement
that was mutually acceptable,

01:19:36.600 --> 01:19:39.040
so they're going to
have to go to trial.

01:19:59.120 --> 01:19:59.480
Baidu,

01:19:59.960 --> 01:20:01.680
China's search engine giant,

01:20:02.160 --> 01:20:06.720
has been blamed by Chinese writers
for participating in copyright violation.

01:20:07.280 --> 01:20:11.720
This is because the website offers
free online excerpts of stories and books.

01:20:12.240 --> 01:20:14.120
without the author's
prior approval.

01:20:14.560 --> 01:20:18.640
I think very late March
or early April of 2011,

01:20:19.080 --> 01:20:23.155
we purged the site of about
2.8 million files that we believed

01:20:23.179 --> 01:20:27.120
might be copyright infringing
within a period of 72 hours.

01:20:27.320 --> 01:20:29.960
I think a good number of them
were books or chapters of books.

01:20:30.600 --> 01:20:34.717
We then implemented a rule
where no one could upload anything

01:20:34.741 --> 01:20:38.720
of more than 1,000 Chinese
characters without it being...

01:20:39.800 --> 01:20:42.162
Manually inspected for
copyright infringement or

01:20:42.186 --> 01:20:44.800
automatically inspected
for copyright infringement.

01:20:45.320 --> 01:20:48.868
The problem is that then people
started uploading parts of books

01:20:48.892 --> 01:20:52.440
in 1,000 character increments
so that they would avoid detection.

01:20:53.040 --> 01:20:56.400
So there are always people
who want to abuse the system.

01:21:00.880 --> 01:21:02.640
The question is,

01:21:03.160 --> 01:21:07.680
has Google already been able
to make its search engine better?

01:21:08.640 --> 01:21:12.520
Because of the Google Books
corpus and the scanning of

01:21:13.080 --> 01:21:14.160
20 million books,

01:21:14.360 --> 01:21:16.040
I think the answer
to that is yes.

01:21:17.040 --> 01:21:20.421
The question of whether
large Internet companies are

01:21:20.445 --> 01:21:23.760
making our lives easier
or gaining power over us,

01:21:23.760 --> 01:21:27.880
I think it presents the kind of false
binary because they're doing both.

01:21:27.920 --> 01:21:28.160
I mean,

01:21:28.240 --> 01:21:29.840
if they were not
making our lives easier,

01:21:29.880 --> 01:21:31.320
no one would be
using their services.

01:21:31.920 --> 01:21:33.640
This is the tricky,

01:21:33.880 --> 01:21:35.480
complicated question
that we'll have to...

01:21:36.000 --> 01:21:37.000
face down the road.

01:21:37.040 --> 01:21:37.280
I mean,

01:21:37.440 --> 01:21:39.160
all of them are
making our lives easier.

01:21:39.160 --> 01:21:39.400
I mean,

01:21:39.440 --> 01:21:41.240
they're making products cheaper.

01:21:41.440 --> 01:21:47.400
They're making our commute
less bothersome and more exciting.

01:21:47.520 --> 01:21:51.148
Google will be supplying us
with glasses that will augment

01:21:51.172 --> 01:21:54.800
reality and tell us about
where our friends are in the city.

01:21:54.920 --> 01:21:55.920
They'll tell us the weather.

01:21:56.000 --> 01:21:57.000
They'll tell us everything.

01:21:57.400 --> 01:21:59.320
The question is what
would the trade-offs be?

01:21:59.600 --> 01:22:02.000
And no one has asked
what happens with all of the

01:22:03.120 --> 01:22:05.480
Information that would
pass through Google glasses.

01:22:05.520 --> 01:22:05.760
I mean,

01:22:05.880 --> 01:22:07.440
surely it will be
stored somewhere.

01:22:07.680 --> 01:22:09.280
Surely Google will
not be discarding it,

01:22:09.320 --> 01:22:11.797
because they will need to
know what it is that I've seen

01:22:11.821 --> 01:22:14.840
yesterday so that they can
customize what I see today even better.

01:22:15.320 --> 01:22:16.400
But then the question is,

01:22:16.880 --> 01:22:21.360
would the National Security Agency be
able to go to Google and ask for that data,

01:22:21.760 --> 01:22:23.920
ask for everything I've seen
through my Google glasses?

01:22:24.440 --> 01:22:25.720
And if that would be the case,

01:22:26.080 --> 01:22:27.000
then the question should be,

01:22:27.400 --> 01:22:29.652
do we actually want
to have a society where

01:22:29.676 --> 01:22:32.360
citizens are burying CCTV
cameras on their heads.

01:22:37.400 --> 01:22:42.040
I can see why many people view Google or
the Internet as some kind of "Big Brother"

01:22:42.440 --> 01:22:50.680
controlling everything, just as Wells
wrote in his science fiction many years ago

01:22:52.360 --> 01:22:54.840
But things are much
simpler thanthat

01:22:55.240 --> 01:22:58.200
The Internet is nothing
more than a medium

01:22:58.600 --> 01:23:04.680
that offers everyone better access to
information, entertainment and knowledge

01:23:24.600 --> 01:23:26.992
Getting to a better system
where people are rewarded

01:23:27.016 --> 01:23:29.360
for their information
contributions to the world,

01:23:29.880 --> 01:23:32.200
getting to that system
from where we are,

01:23:32.920 --> 01:23:36.040
where people are
expected to get by with less,

01:23:36.560 --> 01:23:39.480
that's going to be a hard transition
and they might involve government.

01:23:40.040 --> 01:23:41.600
but they might involve
the big companies.

01:23:41.640 --> 01:23:44.848
And the reason why is that the big
companies like Google and Amazon and

01:23:44.872 --> 01:23:48.080
all the rest are shooting themselves
in the foot with what we're doing,

01:23:48.480 --> 01:23:50.560
because what we're doing
is shrinking the economy.

01:23:50.640 --> 01:23:50.880
I mean,

01:23:50.880 --> 01:23:52.840
I don't know.

01:23:52.840 --> 01:23:59.720
My concern is not so much with the
direction in which Google or Facebook,

01:23:59.720 --> 01:24:00.280
for that matter,

01:24:00.480 --> 01:24:01.560
want to take the world.

01:24:01.720 --> 01:24:05.840
My concern is with the fact that it's Google
and Facebook taking us in that direction.

01:24:18.320 --> 01:24:21.680
You can't just argue that
this serves the common good

01:24:22.080 --> 01:24:24.028
If I go into the Deutsche
Bank, steal all the

01:24:24.052 --> 01:24:26.000
money and distribute
it on the streets and say

01:24:26.400 --> 01:24:30.400
"Look how many unemployed people I am helping!
Look, there aren't any beggars anymore"

01:24:30.800 --> 01:24:34.720
It's still a bank robbery!
Do you understand?

01:24:42.280 --> 01:24:51.080
Our current policy is to open up the library and to
make it part of this really very ambitious project,

01:24:51.360 --> 01:24:53.080
more ambitious I
think than Google's,

01:24:53.560 --> 01:24:54.200
which we call the

01:24:54.600 --> 01:24:56.640
Digital Public
Library of America.

01:24:57.040 --> 01:24:58.640
You know I think that we
owe a great deal to Google.

01:25:01.680 --> 01:25:05.572
I can't imagine that
this digital public library

01:25:05.596 --> 01:25:09.488
of America would ever
have gotten off the ground

01:25:09.512 --> 01:25:13.445
had Google not started
to race ahead with its own

01:25:13.469 --> 01:25:17.320
version of digitization
on this massive scale.

01:25:20.280 --> 01:25:20.680
However,

01:25:21.160 --> 01:25:21.440
you know,

01:25:21.680 --> 01:25:22.040
Google,

01:25:22.840 --> 01:25:24.040
wonderful as it is,

01:25:24.240 --> 01:25:26.560
is not familiar with the books.

01:25:27.080 --> 01:25:27.720
For example,

01:25:28.200 --> 01:25:30.280
Walt Whitman's
famous book of poems,

01:25:30.360 --> 01:25:31.360
Leaves of Grass,

01:25:31.880 --> 01:25:33.720
was catalogued under gardening.

01:25:39.440 --> 01:25:43.760
I was very careful not to come across
as a kind of reactionary French chauvinist

01:25:44.160 --> 01:25:47.192
You know, the Gallic cock
who struts about crowing

01:25:47.216 --> 01:25:50.000
because he is upset
that in the 18th century

01:25:50.400 --> 01:25:54.880
Louis XV abandoned America as
Napoleon did later with Louisiana!

01:25:55.840 --> 01:25:58.428
Some of my opponents
claimed this to be true to

01:25:58.452 --> 01:26:01.040
avoid responding to my
arguments, but it wasn't

01:26:01.440 --> 01:26:07.920
I wanted to launch a European
library project as a response to Google

01:26:08.240 --> 01:26:12.640
which I called 'Europeana

01:26:16.440 --> 01:26:19.688
perfectly compatible
with Europeana and that

01:26:19.712 --> 01:26:22.960
means soon we will
have a worldwide network,

01:26:23.520 --> 01:26:25.440
a gigantic world library.

01:26:28.760 --> 01:26:28.880
H.G.

01:26:29.200 --> 01:26:30.000
Wells'

01:26:30.440 --> 01:26:33.982
view of science and technology
was what sustained him

01:26:34.006 --> 01:26:37.480
and sustained his ideas
throughout his whole life.

01:26:37.560 --> 01:26:40.755
He had this sense that
if only we could get the

01:26:40.779 --> 01:26:44.880
scientists and the technologists
working in the right way.

01:26:45.600 --> 01:26:47.680
We could transform the world.

01:26:48.200 --> 01:26:51.788
And he continued with that
belief up until the absolute

01:26:51.812 --> 01:26:55.200
final disillusionment with
the entire human world,

01:26:56.120 --> 01:26:57.240
with a book which he calls,

01:26:57.320 --> 01:26:57.960
so fittingly,

01:26:58.640 --> 01:27:00.160
Mind at the End of its Tether.

01:27:00.960 --> 01:27:05.248
He felt that the whole evolutionary
process that he had been studying and

01:27:05.272 --> 01:27:09.560
that what he felt was leading us to
something new and wonderful had failed.

01:27:10.680 --> 01:27:14.280
And his last words were
that there was no way out.

01:27:14.920 --> 01:27:16.760
or round or through.

01:27:20.360 --> 01:27:25.760
Our world of self-delusion will perish
amidst its evasions and fortuities.

01:27:27.120 --> 01:27:31.152
It is like a convoy lost
in darkness along an

01:27:31.176 --> 01:27:35.208
unknown rocky coast
with quarrelling pirates

01:27:35.232 --> 01:27:38.994
in the chart room and
savages clambering up

01:27:39.018 --> 01:27:43.320
the sides of the ship
to plunder and do evil as

01:27:43.320 --> 01:27:44.400
the whim may take them.

01:27:45.960 --> 01:27:53.160
That is the rough outline of the more and
more jumbled movie on the screen before us.

01:27:54.720 --> 01:27:56.360
There is no way out,

01:27:57.520 --> 01:27:58.120
or round,

01:27:59.440 --> 01:28:00.000
or through.

